CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional coordinate diagram assembled from three translucent coordinate planes that cut space apart. The vertical axis is labeled z, the axis running to the right is labeled y, and the axis coming forward toward the lower left is labeled x. A pale teal horizontal sheet represents the xy-plane, a peach-colored vertical sheet stretches across the width of the picture, and a blue-violet vertical sheet crosses it at a right angle, all three meeting at the origin. Text set into the wedges between the planes gives the sign pattern of the coordinates there: the near upper wedge carries a bold roman numeral I above the triple (+, +, +), and the surrounding regions are marked (-, -, +), (+, -, +), (-, +, +), (-, +, -), (+, -, -), and (+, +, -), the eighth region being hidden behind the sheets. Portions of the axes drawn as dashed lines indicate where they pass behind a plane. Labeling each region by its signs lets the reader read off directly which side of every coordinate plane that region lies on. The picture carries the familiar division of the plane into four quadrants over into the division of space into eight octants.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - pale teal translucent horizontal sheet = xy-plane (square, centered on origin)
  - peach translucent vertical sheet = yz-plane (spans left-right across the frame, seen nearly face-on)
  - blue-violet translucent vertical sheet = xz-plane (recedes from lower-left front to upper-right back)
  - z-axis: vertical black line through origin with arrowhead at top, label 'z' (italic bold) above arrow
  - y-axis: black line to the right with arrowhead, label 'y' (italic bold) right of arrow
  - x-axis: black line toward viewer lower-left with arrowhead, label 'x' (italic bold) below arrow
  - dashed grey segments of the axes where they pass behind a translucent plane (negative y behind the blue sheet, negative x behind the blue/peach sheets, negative z below the teal sheet)
  - bold roman numeral 'I' in the near upper-right wedge
  - octant sign-triple text labels: (+,+,+) under the 'I', (-,-,+) top center above z, (+,-,+) upper left, (-,+,+) upper right, (+,-,-) lower left, (+,+,-) lower center, (-,+,-) lower right, (-,-,-) hidden behind the sheets
  - thin outline/edge strokes on each plane in a darker tint of its fill color
  - origin point where all three planes and axes meet

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: each plane is a 6 × 6 square centered on the origin (spanning -3..+3 in its two coordinates); axes are drawn from -3.3 to +3.6 with cone arrowheads of length 0.3 on the positive ends only. Orientation as read from the source: +Z is screen-up (z label at top), +Y is screen-right (y label at right), +X projects toward the viewer at screen lower-left (x label just below its arrowhead, slightly left of center-bottom of the axis cross). Plane assignment: teal xy-plane = horizontal sheet, fill #A9D2CE at ~0.45 opacity with edge #4E9A93; peach yz-plane = vertical sheet spanning left-right nearly face-on (contains the y and z axes), fill #F8C4A2 at ~0.55 opacity with edge #E08A5A; blue-violet xz-plane = vertical sheet receding along the x direction (contains the x and z axes), fill #9A9AC8 at ~0.55 opacity with edge #6B6BA8. Axes and arrowheads black (#000); occluded axis stretches drawn as grey dashed (#8A8A8A). Label text dark navy/black ~ figure-caption size. Octant sign-label placements (world positions at radius ~2.2 from origin, pushed to the mid-angle of each wedge): (+,+,+) at (+1.2,+1.2,+1.2) with bold 'I' directly above it; (-,-,+) placed high above the z-arrow near (-1,-1,+3.4); (+,-,+) upper-left at (+1.5,-2.6,+2.2); (-,+,+) upper-right at (-1.5,+2.6,+2.2); (+,-,-) lower-left at (+1.5,-2.6,-2.2); (+,+,-) lower-center at (+1.5,+1.0,-3.0); (-,+,-) lower-right at (-1.5,+2.8,-1.6); (-,-,-) hidden behind the sheets, revealed only on orbit. Octant highlight wedges are the eight axis-aligned boxes of side 3 occupying one sign combination each; highlight raises fill opacity to ~0.35 in gold (#E8B23A) while others stay unfilled. Sign rule to enforce: sign(x) set by side of the yz-plane, sign(y) by side of the xz-plane, sign(z) by side of the xy-plane.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "that the three coordinate planes really are mutually perpendicular and slice space into eight closed wedges — the hidden eighth octant (-,-,-) becomes visible when the view swings behind the sheets"
  },
  {
    "id": "hoverOctant",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "that a region's sign triple is read off directly from which side of each coordinate plane it lies on: hovering a wedge brightens that wedge and expands its (±,±,±) label next to the axes"
  },
  {
    "id": "planeEmphasis",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Emphasize plane",
    "options": [
      "none",
      "xy-plane (z = 0)",
      "xz-plane (y = 0)",
      "yz-plane (x = 0)"
    ],
    "default": "none",
    "teaches": "that crossing one coordinate plane flips exactly one coordinate sign — the chosen plane opaques and the two half-spaces it separates recolor by the sign of that single coordinate"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The three coordinate planes",
    "narration": "Three mutually perpendicular translucent sheets meet at the origin: the teal xy-plane, the peach yz-plane, and the blue-violet xz-plane. Together they cut space into eight octants.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "none"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Above and below the xy-plane",
    "narration": "Emphasizing z = 0 splits space into the four octants with z > 0 above and the four with z < 0 below. Crossing this sheet changes only the third coordinate's sign.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "xy-plane (z = 0)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Front and back of the xz-plane",
    "narration": "Now y = 0 is the dividing sheet: the half-space toward +y (right) and the half-space toward -y recolor. Only the second coordinate's sign flips across it.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "xz-plane (y = 0)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The two sides of the yz-plane",
    "narration": "With x = 0 emphasized, the wedges in front (x > 0) separate from those behind (x < 0). Octant I, labeled (+,+,+), is the near upper wedge on the positive side of all three planes.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "yz-plane (x = 0)"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render planes as THREE.PlaneGeometry with MeshBasicMaterial, transparent:true, depthWrite:false, side:DoubleSide, and explicit renderOrder so the three translucent sheets blend consistently without z-fighting at their intersections; add a LineSegments border per plane. Use an orthographic camera in a standard isometric pose to match the axonometric source. Axes: thin CylinderGeometry or Line2 for constant screen width plus ConeGeometry arrowheads; draw occluded portions as a separate LineDashedMaterial segment (computeLineDistances) so dashes read at all zooms. Octant labels and axis letters as CSS2DRenderer/sprite HTML overlays so they stay upright and legible during orbit; on hover, scale the active label up ~1.4× and bump its font weight. Hover picking uses eight invisible box meshes (one per octant) as raycast targets rather than the thin planes. Plane-emphasis toggle animates opacity: chosen plane to ~0.85, other two to ~0.2, and tints the two half-spaces on either side (e.g. warm for + side, cool for - side) using the corresponding four octant boxes at low opacity.

Follow the interaction plan above.

